        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 12,
        "titulo": "Мне нужно, можно, нельзя",
        "explicacao": "In Russian, many ideas of need and permission do not have a subject like \"I\". The person who needs goes into the dative (мне, тебе, ему...) and the word «нужно» comes after. After it can come an infinitive verb: «Мне нужно работать». If a noun follows, the form of «нужно» agrees with it: «нужен» (masculine), «нужна» (feminine), «нужно» (neuter), «нужны» (plural). But «можно» (it is allowed) and «нельзя» (it is not allowed) never change: they are adverbs.",
        "comparacao": "in English we say \"I need water\", with \"I\" as the subject. In Russian it is \"to me is needed water\" (Мне нужна вода), and «нужна» agrees with the water.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Мне нужно работать.",
            "pt": "I need to work."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Можно воду, пожалуйста?",
            "pt": "May I have some water, please?"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Здесь нельзя громко говорить.",
            "pt": "You must not talk loudly here."
          }
        ],
